{{-- The playground body, shared by /playground/herding and its per-scenario
    pages. Both set `run` in their own server script and include this, so the
    two URLs cannot drift into showing different things. --}}

@if (run)
<section class="mx-auto pb-8 pt-14 px-5 md:pt-20 max-w-[1240px]">
  <span class="chip">Modelled ground, modelled animals</span>
  <h1 class="mt-4 max-w-[24ch] leading-[1.07] text-[2.1rem] sm:text-[2.6rem] lg:text-[3rem]">
    Watch the herding controller work
  </h1>
  <p class="mt-5 max-w-[64ch] leading-relaxed text-[1.075rem] text-[var(--text-2)]">
    This is not an animation of how drone herding might go. It is the same controller that would
    fly the aircraft, run against a modelled paddock, and every position you see is a tick of it.
    Change the scenario and the controller has to solve a different problem. One of them it cannot
    solve, and it stops.
  </p>
</section>

<!-- The scenario picker. Plain links: each one is a page, so it works with
    JavaScript off like the rest of this site. -->
<nav class="mx-auto px-5 max-w-[1240px]" aria-label="Scenario">
  <ul class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 m-0 p-0 list-none">
    @foreach (run.others as other)
      <li>
        @if (other.current)
          <span class="herd-pick herd-pick-on" aria-current="page">{{ other.name }}</span>
        @else
          <a class="herd-pick" href="/playground/herding/{{ other.slug }}">{{ other.name }}</a>
        @endif
      </li>
    @endforeach
  </ul>
</nav>

<section class="mx-auto px-5 py-8 max-w-[1240px]">
  <div class="grid gap-10 lg:gap-12 lg:grid-cols-[1.2fr_1fr] lg:items-start">
    <figure class="m-0 p-4 bg-[var(--panel)] border border-[var(--line)] rounded-[10px]">
      {!! run.map !!}
      <figcaption class="mt-4 text-[0.8rem] text-[var(--text-2)]">
        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-x-5 gap-y-2">
          <span class="flex gap-1.5 items-center"><span class="herd-key-animal legend-swatch"></span>An animal</span>
          <span class="flex gap-1.5 items-center"><span class="herd-key-flighty legend-swatch"></span>A flighty one</span>
          <span class="flex gap-1.5 items-center"><span class="herd-key-drone legend-swatch"></span>The aircraft</span>
          <span class="flex gap-1.5 items-center"><span class="herd-key-standoff legend-swatch"></span>Standoff it is holding</span>
          <span class="flex gap-1.5 items-center"><span class="herd-key-altitude legend-swatch"></span>How much of that is height</span>
          @if (run.world.hazards.length > 0)
            <span class="flex gap-1.5 items-center"><span class="herd-key-hazard legend-swatch"></span>Ground to keep them off</span>
          @endif
        </div>
        <p class="mt-3 max-w-[62ch]">
          The two rings around the aircraft are the whole control story. The outer one is the
          distance it is keeping from the nearest animal; the inner one is how much of that
          distance is height rather than ground. When the mob unsettles, watch the inner ring grow
          while the aircraft stays where it is.
        </p>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div>
      <h2 class="text-[1.55rem]">{{ run.world.name }}</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-[58ch] leading-relaxed text-[0.95rem] text-[var(--text-2)]">
        {{ run.world.brief }}
      </p>

      <div class="mt-6 p-4 rounded-[8px] herd-verdict herd-verdict-{{ run.status }}">
        <p class="m-0 font-semibold text-[0.95rem]">{{ run.verdict }}</p>
        @if (run.abortReason)
          <p class="m-0 mt-2 text-[0.88rem]">{{ run.abortReason }}</p>
        @endif
        @if (run.stragglers > 0)
          <p class="m-0 mt-2 text-[0.88rem]">
            {{ run.stragglers }} left on the old block, reported for somebody to walk out to.
          </p>
        @endif
      </div>

      <h3 class="mt-8 text-[1.05rem]">What it did, against what it was allowed to do</h3>
      <dl class="m-0 mt-4 herd-readings">
        @foreach (run.readings as reading)
          <div class="herd-reading">
            <dt>
              {{ reading.label }}
              @if (reading.limit)
                <span class="herd-limit">limit {{ reading.limit }}</span>
              @endif
            </dt>
            <dd class="telemetry @if (reading.breached) herd-breached @endif">{{ reading.value }}</dd>
            @if (reading.note)
              <p class="herd-note">{{ reading.note }}</p>
            @endif
          </div>
        @endforeach
      </dl>

      <figure class="m-0 mt-8">
        {!! run.trace !!}
        <figcaption class="flex flex-wrap gap-x-5 gap-y-1 mt-2 text-[0.78rem] text-[var(--text-2)]">
          <span class="flex gap-1.5 items-center"><span class="herd-key-speed legend-swatch"></span>Mob speed</span>
          <span class="flex gap-1.5 items-center"><span class="herd-key-height legend-swatch"></span>Aircraft height</span>
          <span>Dotted lines are the limits.</span>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bg-[var(--panel)] border-[var(--line)] border-y">
  <div class="grid gap-8 md:grid-cols-3 mx-auto px-5 py-12 max-w-[1240px]">
    <div>
      <h3 class="text-[1.05rem]">Height comes off first</h3>
      <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-[0.92rem] text-[var(--text-2)]">
        A stockman under pressure can only step back, and a mob that is backed away from stops
        walking. An aircraft can step up instead: it sheds the pressure, takes the rotor noise with
        it, and keeps the position that is doing the driving. So the controller climbs before it
        gives ground, and only gives ground when climbing has not been enough.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3 class="text-[1.05rem]">It watches animals, not averages</h3>
      <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-[0.92rem] text-[var(--text-2)]">
        A hundred head walking at a legal pace with one animal bolting inside them averages out to
        a perfectly compliant move, and the one animal is the one that goes through a fence. Every
        animal is scored on how it is moving: fast relative to the mob, turning hard, or leaving.
        That reading is what sends the aircraft up.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3 class="text-[1.05rem]">Stopping is a result</h3>
      <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-[0.92rem] text-[var(--text-2)]">
        One of these scenarios cannot be solved inside the limits a mob may be driven under, and
        the controller stops and asks for a person. That rung is on this page on purpose. A
        demonstration that only shows the good case is an advertisement, and an advertisement is
        not evidence.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="mx-auto px-5 py-12 max-w-[1240px]">
  <h2 class="text-[1.5rem]">Why you can check this</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 max-w-[64ch] leading-relaxed text-[0.98rem] text-[var(--text-2)]">
    Every scenario is generated deterministically: a fixed seed, no clock, no random numbers. The
    same run produces the same frames every time, which is what lets the figures above be asserted
    in the test suite rather than quoted from a good day. Run it yourself with
    <code class="telemetry">buddy herding:sim --scenario {{ run.world.slug }}</code>.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 max-w-[64ch] leading-relaxed text-[0.98rem] text-[var(--text-2)]">
    The animals are a model, not a claim about livestock. What is real here is the controller: the
    same module decides where the aircraft goes on this page, in the tests and on a flight. See
    <a href="/features/automated-herding">the capability</a> for how a move is planned, authorised
    and flown.
  </p>
</section>
@else
<section class="mx-auto px-5 py-20 max-w-[1240px]">
  <h1 class="text-[2rem]">No such scenario</h1>
  <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--text-2)]">
    That playground does not exist. <a href="/playground/herding">Start from the rotation move</a>.
  </p>
</section>
@endif
